(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_electricchargesandfields=[{id:"neet-2026-q041",pn:41,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Conductors and electrostatic shielding",q:`Which of the following statements are correct?
A. Inside a conductor, the electrostatic field is zero.
B. Electric field at the surface of a charged conductor does not depend on its surface charge density.
C. The interior of a charged conductor can have no excess charge in the static situation.
D. At the surface of a charged conductor, the electrostatic field must be normal to the surface at every point.
E. The electrostatic potential is zero everywhere inside a charged conductor.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["C, D and E only","A, B and D only","A, C and D only","A, C and E only"],c:2,s:`A is correct: the electrostatic field is zero inside a conductor.
B is wrong: the field at the surface is (\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080)n, so it depends on the surface charge density \u03C3.
C is correct: the interior of a charged conductor cannot have excess charge in the static situation.
D is correct: at the surface the field is perpendicular to the surface.
E is wrong: the potential is constant inside, and it can be non-zero.`},{id:"neet-2025-q028",pn:28,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Coulomb's law",q:"Two identical charged conducting spheres A and B have their centres separated by a certain distance. Charge on each sphere is q and the force of repulsion between them is F. A third identical uncharged conducting sphere is brought in contact with sphere A first and then with B and finally removed from both. New force of repulsion between spheres A and B (Radii of A and B are negligible compared to the distance of separation so that for calculating force between them they can be considered as point charges) is best given as:",o:["3F/5","2F/3","F/2","3F/8"],c:3,s:`Initially each sphere has charge q at separation r, so F = Kqq/r\xB2.
The third sphere touches A: each gets q/2, so A has q/2.
Then it (with q/2) touches B (with q): total 3q/2 shared equally, so each gets 3q/4.
Now A has q/2 and B has 3q/4.
F\u2032 = K(q/2)(3q/4)/r\xB2.
F\u2032 = 3F/8.`,sfig:{w:210,h:190,alt:"Two charged spheres A and B a distance r apart: first both q, then A has q/2 and B has 3q/4",d:[["t",34,16,"A","m"],["t",172,16,"B","m"],["c",34,43,18],["c",172,43,18],["l",52,43,154,43],["t",100,57,"r","m"],["t",34,47,"q","ms"],["t",100,57,"r","m"],["t",172,47,"q","ms"],["t",34,136,"A","m"],["t",172,136,"B","m"],["c",34,163,18],["c",172,163,18],["l",52,163,154,163],["t",100,177,"r","m"],["t",34,167,"q/2","ms"],["t",172,167,"3q/4","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Electric dipole in a uniform field",q:"An electric dipole with dipole moment 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 Cm is aligned with the direction of a uniform electric field of magnitude 4 \xD7 10\u2075 N/C. The dipole is then rotated through an angle of 60\xB0 with respect to the electric field. The change in the potential energy of the dipole is:",o:["0.8 J","1.0 J","1.2 J","1.5 J"],c:1,s:`Given |p| = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C m and |E| = 4 \xD7 10\u2075 N/C.
\u03B8\u1D62 = 0\xB0 and \u03B8_f = 60\xB0.
\u0394U = U_f \u2212 U\u1D62.
= \u2212PE cos\u03B8_f + PE cos\u03B8\u1D62.
= PE[cos\u03B8\u1D62 \u2212 cos\u03B8_f].
= 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u2075 [1 \u2212 1/2].
= 10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\u2075 = 1 J.`},{id:"neet-2023-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Torque on dipole",q:"An electric dipole is placed at an angle of 30\xB0 with an electric field of intensity 2 \xD7 10\u2075 NC\u207B\xB9. It experiences a torque equal to 4 Nm. Calculate the magnitude of charge on the dipole, if the dipole length is 2 cm.",o:["2 mC","8 mC","6 mC","4 mC"],c:0,s:`Given E = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 N/C, length = 2 cm = 0.02 m, torque = 4 Nm.
Torque = pE sin\u03B8.
4 = p \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 sin30\xB0, so p = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 C m.
Charge q = p/length = (4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)/0.02 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 C = 2 mC.`},{id:"neet-2023-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Gauss's law",q:"If \u222EE\xB7dS = 0 over a surface, then :",o:["the electric field inside the surface is necessarily uniform.","the number of flux lines entering the surface must be equal to the number of flux lines leaving it.","the magnitude of electric field on the surface is constant.","all the charges must necessarily be inside the surface."],c:1,s:`\u222EE\xB7dS = 0 means the net flux through the surface is zero.
So the number of flux lines entering the surface equals the number leaving it.`},{id:"neet-2022-q047",pn:47,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Field of an electric dipole at large distance",q:"Two point charges \u2212q and +q are placed at a distance of L, as shown in the figure. The magnitude of electric field intensity at a distance R (R >> L) varies as:",o:["1/R\xB3","1/R\u2074","1/R\u2076","1/R\xB2"],c:0,s:`For R >> L the pair acts as an electric dipole.
The field is E = 2p/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3) (on the axis), with p = qL.
So E \u221D 1/R\xB3.`,fig:{w:280,h:88,alt:"A line joining a charge -q at the left end and +q at the right end, with a double-headed arrow below marking the separation L.",d:[["c",30,20,4,"F"],["c",250,20,4,"F"],["l",30,20,250,20],["t",30,44,"\u2212q","m"],["t",250,44,"+q","m"],["l",140,62,32,62,"a"],["l",140,62,248,62,"a"],["t",140,80,"L","m"]]}}]})();
